/**
@mainpage Multi Resolution Molecular Dynamics

MRMD is a stand-alone, open-source C++ package implementing the (Hamiltonian) adaptive resolution
simulation ((H-)AdResS) method, which concurrently couples regions of different resolution — e.g.
atomistic Lennard-Jones and ideal gas — to simulate open molecular systems that exchange particles
and energy with a reservoir.

The software exposes all of its algorithms as composable building blocks,
facilitating both running AdResS simulations and developing the
method itself.

@section mainpage_highlights What you get

- **One code base, many architectures.** All kernels are written against the
  [Kokkos](https://github.com/kokkos/kokkos) performance portability library, so the same source
  runs on serial CPUs, OpenMP and Nvidia or AMD GPUs. See @ref concept.
- **Composable actions on plain data.** Simulation state lives in a handful of containers such as
  mrmd::data::Atoms and mrmd::data::Subdomain; algorithms such as mrmd::action::VelocityVerlet are
  free functions that take those containers and modify them in place. Nothing is hidden behind a
  framework, so new methods can be assembled from existing pieces.
- **AdResS out of the box.** Region weighting (mrmd::weighting_function::Slab,
  mrmd::weighting_function::Spherical), the iterative thermodynamic force
  (mrmd::action::ThermodynamicForce) and multi-resolution ghost exchange
  (mrmd::communication::MultiResGhostLayer) are part of the library, not of a driver script.
  See @ref theory.

@section mainpage_glance MRMD at a glance

A complete ideal gas simulation in the NVE ensemble — this is
@ref tut_00_IdealGas_NVE, trimmed to its essentials:

@code{.cpp}
using namespace mrmd;

// a 100x100x100 box with a ghost layer of thickness 1, filled with 100000 atoms
auto subdomain = data::Subdomain({0_r, 0_r, 0_r}, {100_r, 100_r, 100_r}, 1_r);
auto atoms = util::fillDomainWithAtoms(subdomain, 100000, 1_r, 1_r);

communication::GhostLayer ghostLayer;

for (auto i = 0; i < 2001; ++i)
{
    action::VelocityVerlet::preForceIntegrate(atoms, 0.005_r);
    ghostLayer.exchangeRealAtoms(atoms, subdomain);   // periodic boundary conditions
    // force kernels would go here
    action::VelocityVerlet::postForceIntegrate(atoms, 0.005_r);

    if (i % 100 == 0) io::dumpCSV("atoms_" + std::to_string(i) + ".csv", atoms);
}
@endcode

Adding interactions means adding one more action to the loop; moving to multiple resolutions means
swapping the ghost layer and adding a weighting function. Nothing else about the structure changes.

@section mainpage_next Where to go next

- @subpage getting_started — requirements, build options, picking a Kokkos backend, and running
  your first simulation.
- @subpage concept — the three design principles behind MRMD and a map of the namespaces.
- @subpage theory — the abrupt-interface AdResS scheme and the thermodynamic force iteration.
- @subpage tutorials — eleven worked examples that build on each other, from a minimal ideal gas
  up to a full multi-resolution production workflow.

The API reference is reachable from the sidebar; mrmd::data is a good place to start reading it,
followed by mrmd::action.

